[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'California just did something that could change life for millions of people who\'ve been locked out of the solar boom entirely. And the timing could not be more urgent. This week, the California Legislature passed Senate Bill 868,'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Plug and Play Solar Act, and it\'s now sitting on Governor Newsom\'s desk with a deadline of September 30th to sign it. It\'s almost a certainty that it will be'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'signed. The bill lets anyone plug a solar system of up to 1,200 W straight into a standard wall outlet. You might be thinking to yourself, how'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is this a big deal? Well, have a look at Germany. More than a million people have done this. And it is saving them huge amounts of money. There is no interconnection agreement'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed from the utility, and no local permitting needed. You can just do it. The panels just need to be certified by UL or an equivalent testing lab. The assembly passed 73 to nothing.'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They passed this vote 73 to 0. Complete bipartisan support. The Senate, it was 35 to 1. So, one voted against it, 35 voted for it. That\'s about as'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bipartisan as it absolutely gets on anything in America right now. Why does this matter so much? Because California has roughly 14 million rental units around 40% of all households. And these'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I mean, single-person households are growing faster than any other household type in California. Those people have never been able to touch rooftop solar. They don\'t own a roof, and now here\'s the brutal part,'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which comes from a report the Environmental Working Council group released this month. Extreme and major heat advisory days across California have jumped 55% over the last two decades. And in some'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'regions, as much as 145%. On the Electric Viking website, I\'ll put a link in the description below. We have more articles about electric cars and battery technology'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than any other website that I\'m aware of in the world. So, check that out. Link is in the description. And there you\'ll find our videos and analysis and articles about everything going on in the EV world. Californians are already'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spending $4 billion a year on home air conditioning, adding anywhere from 95 to $800 to their annual bills.'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Almost 7 million ratepayers are in some form of utility debt. Utility debt, 7 million of them. And this is a state where electricity averages around 30 cents a'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kilowatt-hour. The highest in the continental United States. Rates have doubled in a decade. Uh millions of families face a genuinely dangerous choice here. I mean,'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'run the air conditioning and fall further behind on their utility debts, or switch it off and risk heat stroke or having some sort of problem from heat. And it\'s renters who are most exposed.'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Only 64% of two- to four-bedroom apartments in California have air conditioning at all. Compared with 80% of single-family homes. For pregnant women, the elderly, and small children, this is actually a'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'health emergency, not an inconvenience. Balcony solar doesn\'t fix everything, but it directly attacks the problem. An 800-watt kit costs around 500 to 1,000 US dollars, hangs off a railing'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without tools in under 2 hours, and offsets 15 to 25% of a typical apartment\'s consumption. That\'s a big number for a small outlay. At California rates, that\'s $35 to $55 a'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'month or up to 450 US dollars a year, according to Senator Wiener\'s office, with payback in roughly 3 to 5 years. Critically, solar generates the most in'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the middle of a hot afternoon, exactly when the air conditioner is working at its hardest. And when you move, you unplug it and you take it with you. So, if your lease comes up and you\'re'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re going to leave, you can just take it with you. You don\'t have to worry about losing it. If you think this is a niche idea, Germany already has well over a million registered balcony solar systems, and that\'s Germany, not'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'California, where there\'s literally more than twice as much sun. Utah, Virginia, and six other states have legalized balcony solar since 2025. And if Newsom signs, California becomes'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the ninth and by far the biggest market for for balcony solar. The utilities did win one concession, a sunset on the interconnection exemption in January 2030.'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
